WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:02.280
- [Announcer] More than 100 million people

00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:05.850
in the US are experiencing
record high temperatures.

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:09.210
Heat waves are creating a
higher demand for electricity

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:12.390
and straining the already
aging US electric grid,

00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:14.580
increasing the risk of blackouts.

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:16.338
Here's how renewable energy sources,

00:00:16.338 --> 00:00:21.180
like solar, could help maintain
the grid and at what cost.

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:24.030
- A customer-led revolution to solar

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:27.739
and storage is the
ultimate fastest solution

00:00:27.739 --> 00:00:32.200
to climate change and to the
grid demands of the future.

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:35.040
- [Announcer] Mary Powell
is the CEO of SunRun,

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:38.340
a company that provides
residential solar services.

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:40.923
- [Mary] Solar on the customer's
rooftop is interconnected

00:00:40.923 --> 00:00:41.760
with the grid.

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:45.660
So when the customer's home
and using a lot of energy,

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:49.080
it is by definition
supporting the energy need

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:50.730
that is going on right there.

00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:54.540
And when they're not, it is
flowing right onto the grid

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:57.681
as an energy resource to support the grid.

00:00:57.681 --> 00:00:59.400
- [Announcer] Solar panels paired

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:02.190
with home batteries can
be even more beneficial

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:04.213
because the excess energy
stored during the day

00:01:04.213 --> 00:01:07.050
can be sent back to
the grid in the evening

00:01:07.050 --> 00:01:08.678
as solar production goes down.

00:01:08.678 --> 00:01:11.416
And this energy storage can also be used

00:01:11.416 --> 00:01:13.637
to prevent the power
grid from breaking down

00:01:13.637 --> 00:01:16.773
during extreme situations
like a heat wave.

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:20.460
- [Mary] It is stunning
to see this heat wave

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:23.130
that is gripping the United States now.

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:25.290
It can really be the difference

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:27.735
between the grid functioning or not

00:01:27.735 --> 00:01:31.710
during some of these
significant climatic events

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:34.618
that create incredible stress on the grid.

00:01:34.618 --> 00:01:37.650
- [Announcer] The US
Department of Energy said solar

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:40.357
is the fastest growing clean
energy source in the country

00:01:40.357 --> 00:01:43.718
compared to wind, water,
and nuclear power.

00:01:43.718 --> 00:01:45.537
But despite its steady growth,

00:01:45.537 --> 00:01:47.971
solar power comes with limitations

00:01:47.971 --> 00:01:49.320
on how well it can perform

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:51.933
and how much it can
actually support the grid.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:56.460
- Over time, it will not produce as much

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:57.960
as it did initially.

00:01:57.960 --> 00:01:59.550
And so when we work with customers,

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:02.490
what we always build
around is an expected term

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:04.752
of, you know, 25 years.

00:02:04.752 --> 00:02:07.500
- [Announcer] Residential
solar panels degrade

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:10.179
on a median rate of 0.5% a year.

00:02:10.179 --> 00:02:13.594
A drop in efficiency means
less solar energy traveling

00:02:13.594 --> 00:02:16.640
back to the grid to help
out in times of need.

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:20.001
It also means customers
will have to pay more

00:02:20.001 --> 00:02:22.103
for their utilities over time,

00:02:22.103 --> 00:02:25.243
or pay a steep price to
get the panels replaced.

00:02:25.243 --> 00:02:28.680
Temperature can also affect
the panel's efficiency.

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:31.838
Several solar panel providers
state on their websites

00:02:31.838 --> 00:02:35.866
that output efficiency decreases
with extreme temperatures.

00:02:35.866 --> 00:02:39.120
And even though solar
power is considered a type

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:40.170
of green energy.

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:41.850
- What's really cool is yes,

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:44.280
this energy source requires materials.

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:46.470
It does not require fossil fuels.

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:48.570
- [Announcer] Some
materials used in the panels

00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:51.546
such as lead and cadmium can be toxic.

00:02:51.546 --> 00:02:54.030
As demand increases,

00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:57.030
solar companies are
engineering new technology

00:02:57.030 --> 00:03:00.041
to better adapt to extreme
weather conditions.

00:03:00.041 --> 00:03:03.263
Rooftop solar is just one
of the many technologies

00:03:03.263 --> 00:03:07.050
experts say will be needed
to help support the grid.

00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:09.300
- I knew we were never
gonna be able to scale

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:11.439
in time to meet the
demands of climate change

00:03:11.439 --> 00:03:14.490
without, you know, the kind of innovation

00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:17.082
and technological solutions that come

00:03:17.082 --> 00:03:20.219
with a more distributed energy system.

00:03:20.219 --> 00:03:22.636
(calm music)

